  "body": "https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/rendering.html specifies that the center element and the align attribute of div, and table elements other than table, causes an \"align descendants\" behavior on block descendants (which is defined in that section).\n\nThe testcase at https://bug1229401.bmoattachments.org/attachment.cgi?id=8694554 shows that Gecko, IE11, and Chrome 45 also all have this align descendants behavior for p.  I think the spec should describe that; I'm not interested in trying to change the behavior of the align attribute on p for spec purity reasons.\n\nGecko and IE11, but not Chrome 45, also have this behavior for h1..h6.  So the Web at large doesn't seem to depend on one behavior or the other for h1..h6, although it's possible there's some mobile content that depends on the behavior of not doing \"align descendants\".  One advantage of having it for h1..h6 is that there's then one fewer set of align attribute behavior for block-like elements (i.e., those that take the align values left, right, center, middle, and justify).  (table is different because it doesn't do \"align descendants\"; td is different because it takes char and absmiddle... at least in Gecko.)\n",
